# ─── Editors / OS / local scratch ────────────────────────────────────
.idea/
.project
.sass-cache/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
log/
z_temp/
/dist
*.log

# ─── Dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
# NOTE: composer.lock is deliberately NOT ignored — it pins the exact PHP
# dependency versions, so every machine resolves the same tree.

# ─── Source maps (dev-only, rewritten by every `gulp watch`) ─────────
*.map

# ─── Build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RULE: ignore a build directory ONLY if `npm run build` regenerates it
# completely from a tracked source. Anything else stays committed — a
# blanket ignore over a mixed directory silently drops newly-added files,
# and they then go missing on every other machine.
#
#   /assets/build/   ← webpack (wp-scripts) from src/**
#   /assets/atomic/  ← webpack + gulp from inc/AtomicWidgets/**
#
# `build/` used to be written bare, which matches a directory named
# `build` at ANY depth; anchored so it can only ever mean this one.
/assets/build/
/assets/atomic/

# assets/css/ and assets/js/ are NOT ignored, on purpose:
#   1. They are produced by `gulp buildCss` / `gulp buildJs`, which
#      `npm run build` does NOT run (it only runs wp-scripts + the three
#      atomic gulp tasks) — so a fresh clone never regenerates them.
#   2. They also hold vendored third-party libraries with no source at
#      all (select2, typed.js, chroma, progressbar.js, the CodeMirror
#      modes under cs-js/, …). Those can never be rebuilt.
# The old `assets/css/*` / `assets/js/*` lines ignored both kinds
# indiscriminately while 258 of the 273 files there were tracked anyway,
# so all the rule actually did was hide the newest 15.

# ─── Local data ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templates.json
